<article class="env-lemma" id="002W">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/002W">Lemma <span data-tag="002W">4.19.2</span>.</a> Let $\mathcal{I}$ and $\mathcal{J}$ be index categories. Assume that $\mathcal{I}$ is filtered and $\mathcal{J}$ is finite. Let $M : \mathcal{I} \times \mathcal{J} \to \textit{Sets}$, $(i, j) \mapsto M_{i, j}$ be a diagram of diagrams of sets. In this case </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \mathop{\mathrm{colim}}\nolimits _ i \mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits _ j M_{i, j} = \mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits _ j \mathop{\mathrm{colim}}\nolimits _ i M_{i, j}.  \]
</div>
  <p> In particular, colimits over $\mathcal{I}$ commute with finite products, fibre products, and equalizers of sets. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Omitted. In fact, it is a fun exercise to prove that a category is filtered if and only if colimits over the category commute with finite limits (into the category of sets). 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>